## Reached at
  A running Django service served by the development server on `0.0.0.0:8000`,
  working directory `/app`, `PYTHONPATH=/app`,
  `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=tests.testapp.settings`. The implementation is the
  `s3file` package staged under `/app/s3file/` by the image build and
  importable from `/app`:
      import s3file.middleware
      import s3file.forms
      import s3file.storages
  Callers reach it over HTTP at base URL `http://127.0.0.1:8000`, route `/`
  (the upload form and its submission endpoint):
      session.get("http://127.0.0.1:8000/", timeout=20)
      session.post("http://127.0.0.1:8000/", data=<form fields>, timeout=20)
  The middleware is wired into the request path in the app settings, so every
  request to the route passes through it ahead of URL dispatch:
      MIDDLEWARE = MIDDLEWARE_CLASSES = (
          ...,
          "s3file.middleware.S3FileMiddleware",
      )
  The widget is wired by the demo form module, which mixes
  `s3file.forms.S3FileInputMixin` into `django.forms.ClearableFileInput`, so
  each file field rendered on the page is an s3file widget. The middleware is
  the component responsible for resolving the submitted `s3file` paths against
  the storage and exposing the resulting files to the downstream handler on
  `request.FILES`.

## Must provide
  s3file.middleware.S3FileMiddleware   Django middleware class; an instance takes
                                       `get_response` and its `__call__(request)`
                                       runs on every request; referenced by dotted
                                       path in the settings `MIDDLEWARE`
  s3file.forms.S3FileInputMixin        widget mixin class, mixed into
                                       `ClearableFileInput` by the demo form
                                       module; its rendered attributes are read by
                                       callers
  s3file.storages.storage              storage object used by both the widget and
                                       the middleware (a location prefix, an
                                       open-by-relative-path operation, and a
                                       presigned-post client)
  s3file.storages.local_dev            module-level flag imported by the
                                       middleware
  s3file.views.S3MockView              view class imported by the middleware
                                       module; referenced only by its local-dev
                                       branch, which the callers do not hit

  The widget renders, on each file input: a `data-url` attribute (an upload
  URL), a set of `data-*` attributes carrying the fields published by the
  storage'"'"'s presigned-post generator — among them a `data-fields-key` holding
  the upload object key — and an `s3file` CSS class.

## Called as
  GET `/`            no request body; the caller reads, for each s3file widget
                     on the served page, its `data-url`, its `data-*`
                     attributes, and the upload key in `data-fields-key`.

  POST `/`           urlencoded form (the s3file flow). Form fields:
                       s3file                one or more values; each is a string
                                             naming a declared file field
                       <field_name>          one or more values per declared
                                             field; each is a string naming a path
                       <field_name>-<suffix> one POST field per `data-<suffix>`
                                             attribute rendered on that field'"'"'s
                                             widget, carrying that attribute'"'"'s
                                             string value
                     The page'"'"'s rendered hidden fields (a CSRF token field among
                     them) are also forwarded as form values.

  POST `/`           multipart/form-data (the plain-upload flow). One file part
  (multipart)        named after a file field, carrying a (filename: string,
                     content: bytes, content-type: string) tuple; no `s3file`
                     field present.

## Setup required
  - The Django service must already be up and answering on
    `http://127.0.0.1:8000`; the caller blocks until it does. It is started
    from `/app` via the dev server (`python manage.py runserver 0.0.0.0:8000`)
    after `python manage.py migrate --run-syncdb`.
  - `s3file.middleware.S3FileMiddleware` must be present in the settings
    `MIDDLEWARE`/`MIDDLEWARE_CLASSES`, and `S3FileInputMixin` must be mixed
    into the demo form'"'"'s file widget, so requests and rendered widgets actually
    traverse the target rather than bypassing it.
  - Working directory `/app` with `PYTHONPATH=/app` so `s3file` and
    `tests.testapp` import.
  - The storage backs onto the local filesystem: the configured media root
    (`MEDIA_ROOT`, a temporary directory created at startup) combined with the
    `AWS_LOCATION` prefix. The caller places files there out-of-band (directly
    on disk) before submitting their paths, and reads the `AWS_LOCATION` and
    `MEDIA_ROOT` values from the app settings module in a subprocess to know
    where.

## Test harness boundary
  Not part of the implementation, and not rebuilt:
  - A pytest runner drives everything over HTTP through the `requests` library.
  - A shared helper on the calling side parses the served HTML form page to
    extract each widget'"'"'s published `data-*` attributes and upload key, stages
    files directly on the filesystem under candidate media roots, builds POST
    payloads that mirror the widget'"'"'s published contract, and reads
    `AWS_LOCATION`/`MEDIA_ROOT` from the settings module in a `python -c`
    subprocess.
  - The Django development server (`manage.py runserver`) is only the transport
    that carries the HTTP requests to the middleware.
  - The demo app that wires the target into a runnable site — its urls, views,
    forms, models, settings, and template, plus `manage.py` and the entrypoint
    script — is provided scaffolding. Its route handler is what reads
    `request.FILES` to build its response; that consumption belongs to the
    caller side.
