- **Observable behavior:** When a patch has one or more rejected fragments, the region writes a reject artifact beside the patched file recording exactly the rejected fragments. When the patch has no rejected fragments, it reports a clean application (if verbosity allows) and produces no artifact.
- **Inputs:** `state` (an `apply_state`, read for `apply_verbosity` and for `say_patch_name`/`fprintf_ln`/`warning`/`die`/`error_errno` output routing) and `patch` (read for `new_name` and the `fragments` linked list). From each fragment it reads `rejected` (flag), `patch` (pointer to the fragment'"'"'s verbatim text), `size` (its byte length), and `next` (link). It does not mutate `state` or `patch`.
- **Outputs and errors:** Returns `0` when the patch had no rejected fragments (no artifact written). Returns `-1` when a reject artifact was written, which `write_out_results` interprets as "this patch had rejects" (it sets `errs = 1`). Returns `error_errno(...)` (nonzero) when the artifact file cannot be prepared for writing. The artifact'"'"'s first line is exactly `diff a/<new_name> b/<new_name>\t(rejected hunks)\n`; then, in fragment order, the verbatim text of each rejected fragment (`<size>` bytes from `frag->patch`), with a single trailing `'"'"'\n'"'"'` appended only when that fragment'"'"'s last byte is not already `'"'"'\n'"'"'`. Fragments with `rejected` false are skipped in the body. An absent `new_name` on a patch that has rejects causes `die` (process abort).
- **Side effects and interactions:** Creates a regular file at the path `<new_name>` with the suffix `.rej` appended (for a patch to `file.t`, the artifact is `file.t.rej` in the working tree), writes the header and rejected-fragment text to it, then closes it. Emits diagnostic messages to `stderr` (clean-hunk/rejected-hunk notices and the "Applying patch with N rejects" line) only when `apply_verbosity` permits. The artifact path is computed by copying `strlen(new_name)` bytes of `new_name` then the four-byte suffix `.rej` plus terminator into a `PATH_MAX` buffer; if the name would overflow, it is truncated to fit and a warning is emitted. The path construction and the file-open mechanism are internal to the region; only the resulting path and content are observable to the caller.
- **Required sequencing and state:** Called once per patch during phase 1 of `write_out_results`, after cleanly-applicable hunks have already been written to the working tree; the rejected fragments are by then flagged on their `struct fragment` entries. The `-1` return drives `write_out_results` to set the overall error flag rather than abort the loop.
- **Region boundary:** Self-contained. The range spans the whole `write_out_one_reject` definition (opening signature line through the closing `}`). The retained text immediately above is the end of the preceding function and the retained text immediately below is a comment block opening the next function; neither intrudes into this declaration. The replacement must provide a complete, balanced function definition; no tokens are left open or owed.
