```
- **Required interface:** `func VerifyToken(realToken, sentToken string) bool` — package-level export of package `nosurf` (`/app/nosurf/token.go`). Reached by two compiled consumers that link `/app/nosurf` via a `go.mod` `replace`: the demo service'"'"'s `POST /submit` handler calls `nosurf.VerifyToken(cookieToken, sentToken)` (`cookieToken` = `csrf_token` cookie value; `sentToken` = `X-CSRF-Token` header value, else the `csrf_token` form-field value), and a separate Go program'"'"'s `main` calls `nosurf.VerifyToken(os.Args[1], os.Args[2])`. The package'"'"'s own `handler_test.go` also calls `VerifyToken(Token(r), keepToken)`. The name, parameter order (`realToken` then `sentToken`), types (`string, string`), and return type (`bool`) are fixed by these callers.
- **Observable behavior:** Verifies whether the submitted token matches the real (cookie) token. Each argument is interpreted as a standard-base64-encoded token. A 64-byte decoded value is the masked form and is reduced to its 32-byte unmasked form before comparison; no reduction is applied to a decoded value of any other length. On the path that returns `true`, the two resulting byte slices are of equal length and byte-for-byte identical; a pair whose two post-processing values are byte-identical and of the token length (`tokenLength`) returns `true`.
- **Inputs:** `realToken string` (1st positional; the cookie/"real" token) and `sentToken string` (2nd positional; the submitted/"sent" token). Each is an arbitrary string; both are base64-encoded representations of token material when produced by the library.
- **Outputs and errors:** Returns `bool`. A `true` result requires the two post-processing byte slices to be of equal length and byte-for-byte identical; a pair whose two post-processing values are byte-identical and of the token length (`tokenLength`) yields `true`. No error return; no panic in this function.
- **Side effects and interactions:** Reads no global state and mutates nothing. Invokes three package-internal/standard-library facilities: a base64-decode helper (returns `nil` on a string that is not valid standard base64), the constant `tokenLength` (value `32`), and `unmaskToken` — a package-private function defined in the retained sibling `crypto.go` that reduces a 64-byte slice to its 32-byte token half by XORing the second half with the first. A replacement may use that existing package function for unmasking or provide its own; only the 64→32 reduction outcome is observable. The constant-time comparison routine is not externally bound; the check used to compare the two byte slices may be any check that yields `true` only for equal-length, byte-identical slices.
- **Required sequencing and state:** Per argument: decode first, then (only when the decoded length is exactly `2*tokenLength`, i.e. 64) unmask to the 32-byte form; lengths other than 64 are passed through unchanged. The two resulting slices are compared only after both arguments have been processed. These two arguments are processed independently; no cross-argument ordering is observable. Runs only after package `init()` has completed at load.
- **Region boundary:** `Self-contained`. The region spans the whole `VerifyToken` function declaration: it begins at the `func` keyword on its own line and ends with the function'"'"'s closing brace, with no declaration opened by retained text above the region that the region must close, and no declaration left open below. The retained text immediately above is the function'"'"'s own doc comment (which belongs to the function and is restored with it); the retained text immediately below is the next separate declaration (`func verifyToken`). No name introduced by this region is referenced by the retained text below at the region'"'"'s last line.
```

