## Reached at
  A plain PHP function library inside the application tree at /app, staged into the
  image by the build. Two routes, both used by the functional callers and by every
  other caller that measures this code:

  1. CLI route. The system PHP CLI evaluates the app'"'"'s own source file by absolute
     path — no working-directory dependence, no include_path dependence:
         /usr/local/bin/php -r "require_once('"'"'/app/app/function/file.function.php'"'"'); <code>"
     `<code>` calls the functions directly and `echo`es them.

  2. HTTP route. The application is served live by the built-in server started by the
     container entrypoint:
         php -S 0.0.0.0:8080 -t /app
     working directory /app, so /app is the web document root. Callers exercise
     get_path_ext() through this server by writing their own probe script into the
     document root (a file named `.*.php` directly under /app) and requesting it:
         GET http://localhost:8080/.vsb_ext_probe.php?n=<filename>
     The probe itself does `require_once('"'"'/app/app/function/file.function.php'"'"');`
     and passes `$_GET['"'"'n'"'"']` to get_path_ext(). The probe script is caller-written
     on every run — it is not part of the implementation and must not be pre-authored
     or shadowed by it; /app must stay the served document root so a caller-written
     script there is executed by the server.

## Must provide
  /app/app/function/file.function.php    PHP source file, loadable by require_once
                                         under both the CLI and the web SAPI
  get_path_ext                           global PHP function declared by that file
  get_path_this                          global PHP function declared by that file
  get_path_father                        global PHP function declared by that file
  /app/index.php                         application entry point, served at
                                         GET /index.php
  /app/plugins/yzOffice/app.php          present on disk
  /app/plugins/yzOffice/php/yzOffice.class.php   present on disk

## Called as
  get_path_ext($name)      $name — 1st positional argument; a string. Callers pass
                           plain filenames, names containing one or more dots,
                           names with no dot, dotfile-style names, names with
                           directory components, mixed-case names, and names
                           containing spaces, punctuation, or control characters.
  get_path_this($path)     $path — 1st positional argument; a string path.
  get_path_father($path)   $path — 1st positional argument; a string path.

  PHP function names are case-insensitive, but callers resolve get_path_ext by
  exact provenance as well as by name: they instantiate
  `new ReflectionFunction('"'"'get_path_ext'"'"')` and require the reported defining file
  (its realpath) to lie under /app/. The CLI and web routes must therefore resolve
  to the same definition inside the app tree — a definition loaded from elsewhere
  (auto_prepend_file, include_path, or any out-of-tree copy) is not reached.

## Setup required
  The web server must be running and answering on the APP_URL address (default
  http://localhost:8080; `php -S 0.0.0.0:8080 -t /app` under /app satisfies it)
  before the HTTP-route calls land. The CLI route needs only the PHP CLI
  interpreter at /usr/local/bin/php (the image'"'"'s PHP 7.4 CLI with curl, zip, gd)
  and the source file at the absolute path above. The data and config directories
  under /app are writable (the build makes them mode 777); the web server user
  must be able to read /app and create files at its root.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner. For the CLI route it executes
  `/usr/local/bin/php -r "require_once('"'"'/app/app/function/file.function.php'"'"'); ..."`
  through subprocess and inspects captured stdout. For the HTTP route it writes its
  own probe PHP file into /app on every run and requests it through `requests`
  against the base URL above. The runner, the probe script, the subprocess driver,
  and the requests client all belong to the caller, not to the implementation — the
  engineer rebuilds only the app tree under /app, the file that defines the
  functions, and the entrypoint-served application.
