  october/cms and october/system are removed, so those modules live inside the tree
  under modules/ and are project-owned source). Callers read APP_PATH (default
  /var/www/html) and APP_URL (default http://localhost) from the environment and
  reach the tree through two channels.

  1. In-process, through the PHP CLI interpreter, with working directory
     /var/www/html. Every in-process caller boots the same way:

         require '"'"'/var/www/html/bootstrap/autoload.php'"'"';
         $app = require_once '"'"'/var/www/html/bootstrap/app.php'"'"';
         $kernel = $app->make(Illuminate\Contracts\Console\Kernel::class);
         $kernel->bootstrap();

     then instantiates the two module service providers on the booted container and
     drives the backend-permission registration each one owns:

         $sp = new \Backend\ServiceProvider($app);      // and likewise \System\ServiceProvider
         $m  = (new ReflectionClass($sp))->getMethod('"'"'registerBackendPermissions'"'"');
         $m->setAccessible(true);
         $m->invoke($sp);

     and then reads role/permission resolution through the application'"'"'s own
     authorization manager:

         $manager = \Backend\Classes\AuthManager::instance();

  2. Over HTTP, through the Apache-served application at APP_URL (port 80, document
     root /var/www/html, started by apache2-foreground). The backend is mounted
     behind the backend URI segment (CMS_BACKEND_URI=/backend), so backend-module
     controllers answer under /backend/backend/<controller> and system-module
     controllers under /backend/system/<controller>.

## Must provide
  Classes (each must resolve through the application'"'"'s autoloader to a file inside
  /var/www/html; callers read back the defining file of each and treat a location
  outside the tree as a broken setup):
    \Backend\ServiceProvider        class; constructor takes the application container;
                                    declares a registerBackendPermissions method that
                                    callers invoke reflectively
    \System\ServiceProvider         class; same shape as \Backend\ServiceProvider
    \Backend\Classes\AuthManager    class with a static instance() and instance methods
                                    listPermissions() and listPermissionsForRole(...)
    \Backend\Models\UserRole        model; callers query it by its code column
    \Backend\Models\User            soft-deleting model; callers set login, email,
                                    password, password_confirmation, first_name,
                                    last_name, is_superuser, is_activated, role_id and
                                    save it, and look existing rows up by login

  Files:
    /var/www/html/modules/backend/ServiceProvider.php    (php -l is run on it)
    /var/www/html/modules/system/ServiceProvider.php     (php -l is run on it)
    /var/www/html/bootstrap/autoload.php
    /var/www/html/bootstrap/app.php
    /var/www/html/artisan                                (run as php /var/www/html/artisan)

  Permission registry entries — after the two registerBackendPermissions invocations,
  callers enumerate the registry and look these codes up by name:
    backend.access_dashboard
    backend.impersonate_users
    backend.manage_default_dashboard
    backend.manage_preferences
    backend.manage_users
    system.access_logs
    system.manage_mail_settings
    system.manage_mail_templates
    system.manage_updates

  Role records — callers look roles up by these code values in Backend\Models\UserRole
  and stop if a code has no record:
    developer
    publisher

  HTTP routes served by the application (all GET unless noted):
    /backend/backend/auth/signin     GET and POST — the backend sign-in page and the
                                     sign-in form post
    /backend/backend                 backend entry point
    /backend/backend/users           backend administrators page
    /backend/system/updates
    /backend/system/eventlogs
    /backend/system/mailtemplates
    /                                frontend

## Called as
  In-process permission surface (PHP, cwd /var/www/html):
    new \Backend\ServiceProvider($app)        $app — 1st constructor argument; the
                                              container object yielded by
                                              bootstrap/app.php
    $m->invoke($sp)                           no arguments; $m is the reflected
                                              registerBackendPermissions method of $sp
    \Backend\Classes\AuthManager::instance()  no arguments
    $manager->listPermissions()               no arguments
    $manager->listPermissionsForRole('"'"'publisher'"'"', true)
    $manager->listPermissionsForRole('"'"'developer'"'"', true)
                                              1st positional argument — a role code, a
                                              string; 2nd positional argument — a
                                              boolean

  CLI:
    php -l /var/www/html/modules/backend/ServiceProvider.php
    php -l /var/www/html/modules/system/ServiceProvider.php
    php /var/www/html/artisan --version

  Model surface used by a caller-side account driver before HTTP contact:
    UserRole::where('"'"'code'"'"', $code)->first()          $code — a string
    User::withTrashed()->where('"'"'login'"'"', $login)->first()   $login — a string
    (a new User row is then populated with the attributes listed under Must provide
    and saved)

  HTTP surface (base URL APP_URL):
    GET  {APP_URL}/                                   (redirects followed)
    GET  {APP_URL}/backend/backend/auth/signin        (redirects followed)
    POST {APP_URL}/backend/backend/auth/signin
         form fields: login — a string; password — a string; postback — a string;
         _token — a string; _session_key — a string (the caller takes the _token and
         _session_key strings from the sign-in form fields on the GET'"'"'d page)
    GET  {APP_URL}/backend/backend                    (redirects followed)
    GET  {APP_URL}/backend/backend/users              (some callers disable redirects)
    GET  {APP_URL}/backend/system/updates             (some callers disable redirects)
    GET  {APP_URL}/backend/system/eventlogs           (redirects followed)
    GET  {APP_URL}/backend/system/mailtemplates       (redirects followed)

## Setup required
  - Apache must be serving the application tree at APP_URL on port 80 before any
    request is made; the caller-side runner polls GET APP_URL until it answers.
  - APP_PATH must name the application tree; in-process callers run php <script>
    with cwd = APP_PATH, and some caller scripts are written inside the tree under
    {APP_PATH}/storage/, so storage/ must be writable.
  - The application must be installed and migrated: composer dependencies present
    under vendor/ (the Laravel framework and october/rain), the .env in place with
    the sqlite database at storage/database.sqlite, and the role records for the
    developer and publisher codes already present in Backend\Models\UserRole.
  - The container must bind Illuminate\Contracts\Console\Kernel so that
    $app->make(Illuminate\Contracts\Console\Kernel::class)->bootstrap() is the entry
    the in-process callers use.
  - Both channels reach the same application state: in-process resolution through the
    CLI interpreter and requests served by Apache.

## Test harness boundary
  The pytest runner, its session-scoped fixtures, the readiness poll, the PHP probe
  scripts the runner writes to temporary files (system temp directory, and for some
  bootstrap checks under {APP_PATH}/storage/) and executes through the php
  interpreter, the caller-side account-provisioning script, the requests.Session
  sign-in driver, and php -l all belong to the calling side, as does the php
  interpreter itself. The reflective drive into registerBackendPermissions is harness
  code; the registration it drives, the AuthManager resolution it reads, the User and
  UserRole models it writes, and the backend pages Apache serves are the
  implementation. The engineer does not rebuild the runner, the probe scripts, the
  provisioning script, or the sign-in driver.
